# Recommended Pattern for Streaming React Server Components with Suspense

Based on the React codebase, here's the recommended pattern for streaming React Server Components with Suspense:

## Server-Side Streaming APIs

React provides two primary streaming APIs exported from `react-dom/server`:

```javascript
// For Node.js environments
import { renderToPipeableStream } from 'react-dom/server';

// For modern edge runtime environments (Deno, Cloudflare Workers)
import { renderToReadableStream } from 'react-dom/server';
```

**Source:** `facebook/react/CHANGELOG.md` - These APIs have "full support for streaming Suspense on the server"

## Basic Streaming Pattern with Suspense

```javascript
// Server Component
async function DataComponent() {
  const data = await fetchData(); // This can be async!
  return <div>{data}</div>;
}

// App with Suspense boundary
function App() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <DataComponent />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

## Node.js Streaming Example

```javascript
import { renderToPipeableStream } from 'react-dom/server';

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  const { pipe } = renderToPipeableStream(<App />, {
    onShellReady() {
      // Start streaming the shell immediately
      res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/html');
      pipe(res);
    },
    onError(error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  });
});
```

## Edge Runtime Streaming Example

```javascript
import { renderToReadableStream } from 'react-dom/server';

export default async function handler(request) {
  const stream = await renderToReadableStream(<App />, {
    onError(error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  });

  return new Response(stream, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' },
  });
}
```

## Key Benefits of This Pattern

1. **Progressive Rendering**: The shell HTML is sent immediately while data loads
2. **Automatic Fallback Handling**: Suspense boundaries emit fallback HTML when components suspend
3. **Client Hydration**: Suspended content is automatically retried and hydrated on the client

## Important Notes

- **Avoid `renderToString`**: While it still works, it's discouraged. As noted in `CHANGELOG.md`: "it will emit the fallback HTML for the closest `<Suspense>` boundary and then retry rendering the same content on the client. It is still recommended that you switch to a streaming API like `renderToPipeableStream` or `renderToReadableStream` instead."

- **Static Site Generation**: For SSG use cases, React now provides `prerender` and `prerenderToNodeStream` APIs that "wait for data to load for HTML generation" (`facebook/react/CHANGELOG.md`)

This streaming pattern ensures optimal performance by sending HTML to the client as soon as possible while data loads in the background.

react-core.baseline:
// server.js (Node + express example using React 18 streaming)
const express = require('express');
const React = require('react');
const { renderToPipeableStream } = require('react-dom/server');
const App = require('./App.server').default;

const app = express();
app.use('/client.js', express.static('dist/client.js')); // your bundled client

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/html; charset=utf-8');

  const { pipe, abort } = renderToPipeableStream(React.createElement(App), {
    onShellReady() {
      // send the shell immediately (html up to the root container)
      res.write(`<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>RSC + Suspense</title></head><body>`);
      res.write(`<div id="root">`);
      pipe(res); // start streaming the React output into the root
    },
    onShellError(err) {
      res.statusCode = 500;
      res.send(`<!doctype html><p>Server rendering failed</p><pre>${err.message}</pre>`);
    },
    onAllReady() {
      // all Suspense boundaries resolved and stream finished; close HTML
      res.write(`</div><script src="/client.js"></script></body></html>`);
      res.end();
    },
    onError(err) {
      console.error(err);
    }
  });

  // safety timeout
  setTimeout(() => abort(), 10000);
});

app.listen(3000);

// App.server.jsx (Server Component using Suspense)
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import Slow from './Slow.server';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Server Component shell</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading slow part…</div>}>
        <Slow />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  );
}

// Slow.server.jsx (async server component)
import React from 'react';

async function fetchSlow() {
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 2000));
  return 'Slow data loaded';
}

export default async function Slow() {
  const data = await fetchSlow();
  return <div>{data}</div>;
}

// client.js (client hydration entry)
import React from 'react';
import { hydrateRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import ClientApp from './App.client'; // a client-compatible entry matching the server-rendered markup

hydrateRoot(document.getElementById('root'), <ClientApp />);

Notes:
- The server component (Slow.server.jsx) can be async and will suspend; Suspense provides a fallback while it resolves.
- renderToPipeableStream streams the shell immediately (onShellReady) and progressively fills Suspense boundaries as they resolve.
- Use hydrateRoot on the client to attach React to the streamed HTML so interactive client components take over.

